<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Stolbovo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1617">1617</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Stolbovo · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingrian-war">Ingrian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Stolbovo, signed on 27 February 1617, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingrian-war">Ingrian War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Sweden returned Novgorod, which it had occupied, but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Kexholm</a>, cutting Russia off from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. The treaty was a foundation of Swedish dominance in the Baltic and stood until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> reversed it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo.jpg" alt="Treaty of Stolbovo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1617,_May_1._Gustav_Adolf%27s_ratification_of_Russia-Sweden_peace_(RGADA).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
